[xUnit.net 00:00:50.64]       OPEN RULING — Meals owner. A shared mailbox can claim another employee's invitation because the confirmed-email match does not require the address to identify ONE account (Identity's RequireUniqueEmail is unset and AspNetUsers.Email carries no unique index). Four remedies are written up — enforce address uniqueness, bind the invitation to an account id at issue, refuse an address resolving to more than one account, or require a second channel — and choosing between them changes what a company admin must type, so it is not a test's call. This fact states the property every remedy must satisfy and is unskipped the day one lands; A_shared_mailbox_colleague_can_still_take_the_membership_the_company_meant_for_someone_else fails on that same day and is what makes this skip impossible to forget.
Test 'WebApi.Tests.Meals.MealsInvitationIdentityBindingTests.Only_the_account_the_company_meant_ends_up_holding_the_membership' was skipped in the test run.
[xUnit.net 00:02:07.71]       JOURNEY-GAP: the CONSUMER employee-ordering surface (payer strip, eligibility state, the CompanyAccount payment option, reduce-or-switch) is a separate consumer-app lane. The backend attribution seam it drives — quote → CartService.PromoteToOrder(CompanyAccount) bind → capture — is proven end-to-end in MealsPilotFullLoopJourneyTests and MealsOrderLoopJourneyTests.
[xUnit.net 00:02:07.71]       JOURNEY-GAP: the human-readable statement PDF export is a documented fast-follow (CSV ships, spec §13.3). The gap BOUNDARY is asserted green in MealsPilotFullLoopJourneyTests (a pdf request returns the typed meals.export-format-unsupported 400); the byte-deterministic PDF renderer is a later wave.
